  "tips": "**Adjective Patterns (Mishkalim):**\n\n| Pattern | Example | Meaning | Usage |\n|---------|---------|---------|-------|\n| קַטְלָן | שַׁכְחָן (forgetful) | tendency / habitual trait | Someone who tends to do something |\n| קַטִּיל | אָכִיל (edible), שָׁבִיר (fragile) | passive quality / '-able' | Can be done to, quality of being |\n| קַטְלָנִי | אַכְזָרִי (cruel) | characteristic adjective | Describing an inherent quality |\n\n**Verbal Noun Patterns:**\n\n| Pattern | Binyan | Example | Meaning |\n|---------|--------|---------|--------|\n| קִיטּוּל | Pi'el | יִיצּוּר (production), לִיטּוּשׁ (polishing), שִׁיפּוּר (improvement), גִּידּוּל (growth) | Action/process nouns from Pi'el verbs |\n| הַקְטָלָה | Hif'il | הַדְרָכָה (training), הַנְחָיָה (guideline) | Action nouns from Hif'il verbs |\n| הִתְקַטְּלוּת | Hitpa'el | הִתְנַהֲלוּת (conduct), הִתְפַּתְּחוּת (development) | Process/state nouns from Hitpa'el verbs |\n\n**Key insight:** The **קַטִּיל** pattern is like the English suffix '-able/-ible': אָכִיל (edible = can be eaten), שָׁבִיר (fragile = can be broken). The **קַטְלָן** pattern describes someone with a habitual tendency: שַׁכְחָן (forgetful one), רַגְזָן (hot-tempered one)."
}
